# Invoice workflow (Mermaid)

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Create client] --> B[Create resource and client assignment]
    B --> C[Create contract with rate and effective dates]
    C --> D[Enter timesheet for billing period]
    D --> E{Timesheet reviewed?}
    E -- No --> D
    E -- Yes --> F[Approve timesheet]
    F --> G[Billing specialist selects client and date range]
    G --> H{Approved, uninvoiced timesheets exist?}
    H -- No --> X[Show validation message]
    H -- Yes --> I{Active contract rate covers service period?}
    I -- No --> Y[Show missing/invalid contract error]
    I -- Yes --> J[Calculate hours × rate and applicable tax]
    J --> K[Create invoice and line snapshots]
    K --> L[Link timesheets to invoice in SQL transaction]
    L --> M[Review invoice]
    M --> N{Approved?}
    N -- No --> O[Keep as Draft / correct data]
    N -- Yes --> P[Approve invoice]
    P --> Q[Generate PDF]
    Q --> R[Send invoice to client - email integration]
    R --> S[Record full or partial payment]
    S --> T{Balance due = 0?}
    T -- Yes --> U[Mark Paid]
    T -- No --> V[Keep balance outstanding / follow up]
```

## Key business rules

1. An invoice can only be generated from approved timesheets that have not already been invoiced.
2. The client on the resource assignment must match the selected invoice client.
3. A valid contract must cover the timesheet service period.
4. Invoice amounts use decimal arithmetic and explicit SQL precision; do not use floating-point types for currency.
5. Invoice lines store the billed resource name, hours, rate, and amount as a historical snapshot.
6. Invoice generation and timesheet linking must be atomic.
7. Payments must be positive and cannot exceed the outstanding balance.
8. Draft and void invoices cannot receive payments.
